\abs{Masanori Adachi}{Tokyo University of Science}
{Curvature restrictions for Levi-flat real hypersurfaces in complex projective planes}
{
We study curvature restrictions of Levi-flat real hypersurfaces in complex projective planes, whose existence is in question. We focus on its totally real Ricci curvature, the Ricci curvature of the real hypersurface in the direction of the Reeb vector field, and show that it cannot be sater than $-4$ along a Levi-flat real hypersurface. We rely on a finiteness theorem for the space of square integrable holomorphic 2-forms on the complement of the Levi-flat real hypersurface, where the curvature plays the role of the size of the infinitesimal holonomy of its Levi foliation. This talk is based on joint work with Judith Brinkschulte.}


\abs{ Junyan Cao}{Jussieu}{
Kodaira dimension of algebraic fiber spaces over abelian varieties or surfaces}
{ Let $p: X\rightarrow Y$ be a fibration between two projective manifolds. The Iitaka conjecture 
states that
$$\kappa (X) \geq \kappa (Y)+\kappa (X/Y) ,$$
where $\kappa (X)$ is the Kodaira dimension of $X$ and $\kappa (X/Y)$ is the Kodaira dimension of the general fiber. By using mainly the recent work of M. P\u{a}un and S. Takayama about the positivity of relatively canonical bundles,  we give a proof of the Iitaka conjecture for algebraic fiber spaces over abelian varieties or over surfaces. This a joint work with M. P\u{a}un.}

\abs{ Tristan Collins}{Harvard University}
{Non-collapsed limits of Calabi-Yau manifolds}
{We will discuss a geometric characterization of classes of positive volume on the boundary of the K\"ahler cone of a compact Kahler manifold. The main ingredient is a numerical characterization of the gaugmented base locush for big and nef $(1,1)$-classes, extending results of Nakamaye, and
 Ein-Lazarsfeld-Mustata-Nakamaye-Popa. We will also discuss further extensions of this notion to big, but not nef, $(1,1)$-classes. As an application, we will discuss optimal regularity for Calabi-Yau metrics as the Kahler class degenerates. This work is joint with Valentino Tosatti.}

\abs{ Tamas Darvas }{University of Maryland}{
Metric geometry on the space of K\"{a}hler metrics }{
abstract: We introduce the $L^p$-Mabuchi Finsler structure on the space of K\"ahler metrics. We argue that the completion of the associated path length metric structures are well known objects from finite energy pluripotential theory. Time permitting we will mention how the use of the arising metric geometry is useful in settling questions about the long time behavior of the Calabi flow and $J$-properness.
}

\abs{Gopal Datt}{University of Delhi}{
Quasinormality in several complex variables
}{
In this talk, we will discuss a renormalization result for quasinormality as well as for weak normality. Roughly speaking it says that for a non quasinormal family of meromorphic mappings, we get a
 non-constant meromorphic map in the limit, after rescaling of the family. We also discuss a quasinormality criterion concerning shared hyper surfaces.
}

\abs{ Philippe Eyssidieux}{Institute Fourier, Grenoble}{
 Viscosity solutions of the K\"{a}hler-Ricci flow and the Song-Tian
canonical metric }{ 
From a joint work with Guedj and Zeriahi.
Studying the K\"ahler-Ricci ow on mildly singular varieties, one is naturally led
to study weak solutions of degenerate parabolic complex Monge-Amp\`{e}re equations.
The purpose of the talk is to explain an approach for degenerate complex Monge-Amp\`{e}re flows based on the ideas of viscosity solutions to degenerate elliptic or and parabolic equations. We apply this method to the the long-term behavior of the K\"{a}hler-Ricci flow, giving an alternate, more general, approoach to Song-Tian's results.}


\abs{ Ryushi Goto}{Osaka University}{
Stratified flat structures on moduli spaces of generalized complex surfaces }{
We show that a logarithmic generalized complex surface with smooth elliptic curve as type changing locus has unobstructed deformations which are parametrized by the second de Rham cohomology group of the complement of type changing locus. Investigating the cases of type changing loci with singularities, we construct moduli spaces of generalized del Pezzo surfaces. 
A moduli space of generalized complex surfaces admits a stratification consisting of strata which are given by type changing locus with a fixed number of singularities such as nodes and cusps.
Then it turns out that a moduli space of generalized complex surfaces has ``stratified flat structure", that is, 
all strata have the flat structure given by an integral lattice the second de Rham cohomology group. 
We also discuss the correspondence between generalized complex surfaces and noncommutaive complex surfaces. }


\abs{ 
Tobias Harz}{POSTEC}{
On smoothing of bounded plurisubharmonic functions }{
I will talk about work in progress concerning the smoothing of bounded (from above) plurisubharmonic functions on unbounded domains in $\mathbb{C}^n$, $n \ge 2$. In particular, I will present the following result. \smallskip

\noindent \textit{There exist a pseudoconvex domain $\Omega \subset \mathbb{C}^2$ and a nonempty open set $U \subset \Omega$ such that the following assertions hold true: 
\begin{enumerate}
 \item There exists a function $\varphi \in \mathcal{PSH}(\Omega) \cap \mathcal{C}^0(\Omega)$ such that $\varphi < 0$ on $\Omega$ and $\varphi$ is strictly plurisubharmonic on $U$.
 \item Every function $\varphi \in \mathcal{PSH}(\Omega) \cap \mathcal{C}^1(\Omega)$ such that $\varphi < 0$ on $\Omega$ is constant on $U$. 
\end{enumerate}}
}


\abs{ Kang-Tae Kim}{POSTECH}{
Genelized Forelli theorems}{
Our study has been inspired by the following

\begin{theorem} (Forelli [2], 1977) If a complex-valued function $f$ from the open unit ball $B^{n}$ in $\mathbb{C}^{n}$ satisfes the conditions
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(A)] $f$ is $C^{\infty}(0)$, i.e., $\forall k$, $\exists U = U(0)$, $f\in  C^{k}(U)$.

\item[(B)] $\zeta\to f(\zeta v)$ is holomorphic in $\zeta \in B^1$ for every $v  \in \mathbb{C}^{n}$ with $\parallel v\parallel  = 1$,
\end{enumerate} 
Then $f : B^{n}\to \mathbb{C}$ is holomorphic.
\end{theorem}

This theorem was often mentioned as impossible to generalize, mainly because they tried to improve (A). Then E. Chirka [1] showed that (B) can be improved, in complex dimension 2, with the more relaxed condition that the function f is holomorphic along a ``singular fibration'' of $B^2$ passing through the origin ``transversely''. Then he posed the question whether his theorem can be proved in all dimensions.

In [3], we obtained the all dimensional theorem predicted by Chirka:

\begin{theorem} (Chirka/Joo-Kim-Schmalz) If a domain $\Omega\subset\mathbb{C}^n$ contains the origin and admits a $C^1$ radial type foliation by holomorphic discs, and if $f\colon\Omega\to\mathbb{C}$ belongs to $C^\infty(0)\cap C^1(\Omega)$ with the restriction to each leaf holomorphic, then $f$ itself is holomorphic.
\end{theorem}
On the other hand, a natural re-interpretation of Forellifs condition (B) is
that $\overline{E}f = 0$ where $E = \sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}}$. With this viewpoint, it is natural to try to replace the condition $(B)$ by 
\begin{equation}\tag{B${}^\prime$}
Xf = 0 
\end{equation}
where $X$ is a holomorphic vector field vanishing at $0$, and ask the following

\medskip
\noindent
{\bf Question} Which X makes the statement ``If $f : B^{n}\to\mathbb{C}$ satisfes (A) and (B${}^\prime$), then f is holomorphic.'' remain valid?
\\


\smallskip

In the series of papers [5, 4], we obtained

\begin{theorem} (Joo-Kim-Poletsky-Schmalz) If a function $F : B^{n}\to \mathbb{C}$ satisfies

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(1)] $f\in C^{\infty}(0)$, and
\item[(2)] $F$ is holomorphic along every complex integral curve of an aligned holomorphic vector field, 
\end{enumerate}
then $F$ is holomorphic on $B^{n}$.
\end{theorem}


\noindent{\bf References}

\noindent
[1] E. Chirka: Variations of Hartogs' theorems, Tr. Mat. Inst. Steklova 253, 232-240 (2006)

\noindent
[2]  F. Forelli: Pluriharmonicity in terms of harmonic slices, Math. Scand. 41, 358-364 (1977)

\noindent
[3] J.-C. Joo, K.-T. Kim, G. Schmalz: Math. Ann. 355-3, 1171-1176 (2013)

\noindent
[4] J.-C. Joo, K.-T. Kim, G. Schmalz: On the generalization of Forelli's theorem, Math. Ann. 2016; To be printed.

\noindent
[5] K.-T. Kim, E. Poletsky, G. Schmalz: Functions holomorphic along holomorphic vector fields, J. Geom. Anal. 19, 655-666 (2009)

}


\abs{ Takayuki Koike }{Kyoto University}{
Higher codimensional Ueda theory for a compact submanifold with unitary 
flat normal bundle }{
We pose a higher-codimensional generalization of Ueda's theory on the 
neighborhood structure of compact complex submanifolds.
Let $Y$ be a compact complex manifold embedded in a complex manifold 
with unitary flat normal bundle.
Our interest is in a sort of the linearizability problem of a 
neighborhood of $Y$.
As a higher-codimensional generalization of Ueda's result,
we give a sufficient condition for the existence of a non-singular 
holomorphic foliation on a neighborhood of $Y$ which includes $Y$ as a 
leaf with unitary-linear holonomy.
We apply this result to the existence problem of a smooth Hermitian 
metric with semi-positive curvature on a nef line bundle.}

