\abs{ Taiji Marugame}{University of Tokyo}{
Constructions of global CR invariants via Cheng-Yau metric }{ 
Strictly pseudoconvex domains in a Stein manifold admit a complete 
K\"ahler-Einstein metric called the Cheng-Yau metric. In this talk, 
we present two methods of constructing global CR invariants of the 
boundary using this metric. One is the renormalized Chern-Gauss-Bonnet
formula, which gives the Burns-Epstein invariant. The other is the 
volume renormalization, which gives the total $Q$-prime curvature. 
This is joint work with K. Hirachi and Y. Matsumoto. 
}

\abs{ Yoshihiko Matsumoto}{Osaka University) }{
Deformation of Einstein metrics and $L^{2}$ cohomology on strictly pseudoconvex domain }
{
Any bounded strictly pseudoconvex domain $\Omega$ of a
 Stein manifold $Y$ carries a complete K\"{a}hler-Einstein metric of negative scalar curvature, which is unique up to homothety, as shown by Cheng and Yau [2]. I will discuss the fact that this Cheng-Yau metric deforms into a family of Einstein metrics on $\Omega$ parametrized by partially integrable CR structures on the boundary (under the technical assumption that the dimension is at least three); this is a generalization of a work of Biquard [1] on the deformations of the metric of the complex hyperbolic space $\mathbb{CH}^n$. The argument is based on the observation that the Cheng-Yau metric is asymptotically complex hyperbolic (ACH) in a sense, and our new metrics fall into the general class of ACH metrics. The theorem is stated as follows.

\vspace{3mm}

{\bf Theorem. }{\em Let $\Omega$ be a smoothly bounded strictly pseudoconvex domain of a Stein manifold Y of dimension $n\ge 3$. Suppose that $J$ is a partially integrable CR structure on the boundary $\partial\Omega$ suffciently close to the one induced by the complex structure of $Y$ in the $C^{2,\alpha}$-topology. Then there exists an Einstein ACH metric $g$ on $\Omega$ with conformal infinity $J$. The metric $g$ is locally unique modulo the action of diffeomorphisms on $\Omega$ inducing the identity on $\partial\Omega$.} 
\vspace{3mm}
 
The proof is given by applying the implicit function theorem. The necessary analysis on the linearized Einstein operator of the Cheng-Yau metric can be reduced to a vanishing result of the $L^{2}$ Dolbeault cohomology with values in the holomorphic tangent bundle, which can be shown by utilizing the asymptotic complex hyperbolicity. Details can be found in [3].

\medskip

\noindent{\bf References}

[1] O. Biquard, M\'etriques d'Einstein asymptotiquement sym\'etriques, Ast\'erisque {\bf 265} (2000), vi+109.

[2] S. Y. Cheng and S. T. Yau, On the existence of a complete K\"ahler metric on noncompact complex manifolds and the regularity of Feffermanfs equation, Comm. Pure Appl. Math. 33 (1980) 507--544.

[3] Y. Matsumoto, Deformation of Einstein metrics and $L^{2}$ cohomology on strictly pseudoconvex domains. arXiv:1603.02216}

\abs{ Thibaut Delcroix}{Institute Fourier, Grenoble}{
 $K$-stability and K\"{a}hler-Einstein metrics on
spherical varieties }{ 
Spherical manifolds form a large class of manifolds with a lot of symmetries.
Examples include toric manifolds, biequivariant group compactifications, homogeneous toric bundles, equivariant compactifications of
symmetric spaces. I will present a criterion for the existence of 
K\"{a}hler-Einstein metrics on Fano spherical manifolds involving only their moment
polytope and valuation cone. Both are classical and central combinatorial
data in the theory of spherical varieties.
The criterion is of combinatorial nature and generalizes previously
known results for subclasses of Fano spherical manifolds, in particular
the criterion I obtained for group compactifications by the continuity
method. It allows to obtain new examples of K\"{a}hler-Einstein manifolds,
examples of Fano manifolds with no K\"{a}hler-Ricci solitons which are not
$K$-semistable, and examples of Fano manifolds with Picard rank one that
are not $K$-semistable.
The proof is obtained via $K$-stability and the equivariant version of
the Yau-Tian-Donaldson conjecture proved by Datar and Sz\'{e}kelyhidi.}






\abs{ Dan Popovici }{Toulouse}{Non-K\"{a}hler mirror symmetry of the Iwasawa manifold}{
This is joint work with Christophe Mourougane. We prove that the 
Iwasawa manifold, a standard non-K\"ahler nilmanifold of complex dimension 
 3, is its own mirror dual in a sense where the standard K\"ahler cone, 
 that is empty in this case, is replaced by the Gauduchon cone. On the 
 complex-structure side of the mirror, we define what we call ``the 
 universal family of essential deformations" of the Iwasawa manifold that 
 is then shown to have a clear geometric meaning including in terms of the 
 degeneration at $E_2$ of the Fr\"olicher spectral sequence. We construct 
 coordinates and Yukawa couplings, as well as a variation of Hodge 
 structures while implicitly proving a local Torelli theorem. On the metric 
 side of the mirror, we construct a variation of Hodge structures 
 parametrised by the complexified Gauduchon cone of the Iwasawa manifold 
 using the sGG property of all the small deformations of this manifold 
 proved in an earlier joint work of one of the authors with L. Ugarte. 
 Finally, we define a mirror map linking the two variations of Hodge 
 structures and we study its properties.}

\abs{ Xu Wang }{Chalmers}
{Relative $\bar{\partial}$-complex and its curvature properties }{ 
We shall show how to use H\"ormander's $L^2$-estimates to study the curvature properties of vector bundles associated to the relative  $\bar{\partial}$-complex. A direct consequence is Yamaguchi's theory on plurisubharmonicity of the Green-operator can be seen as a curvature property of the quotient bundle. Some applications (joint work with Bo Berndtsson) to the curvature properties of the base manifold will also be given.}

\abs{  Katsutoshi Yamanoi }{Osaka Univeristy}{
Pseudo Kobayashi hyperbolicity of subvarieties of general type on abelian varieties  }{
Starting from some basic definitions of complex hyperbolic geometry, we discuss a proof of pseudo Kobayashi hyperbolicity of subvarieties of general type on abelian varieties.}




\abs{ 
Sungmin Yoo}{POSTEC)}{
 A differential geometric analysis of the Bergman representative map
}{
In this talk, I would like to introduce a geometric interpretation of the Bergman representative map. More precisely, I would demonstrate a construction of a locally flat holomorphic affine connection on the holomorphic tangent bundle of a subdomain of a given complex manifold. Its affine exponential map is the inverse of the representative map.
As an application, I would present the following theorem that is a generalization of Lu Qi-Keng's ball theorem: Suppose that there is a locally isometric (w.r.t. the Bergman metric) holomorphic surjective map from a bounded domain to a complex manifold. If the bounded domain has a pole with respect to the holomorphic connection, then the map is a biholomorphism.}



